DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppssttooppnnmm reads a PostScript file as input and produces PBM, PGM, or  PPM
       images  as  output.   This  program simply uses GhostScript to render a
       PostScript file with its PNM device drivers.  If you don't have  Ghost‐
       Script  installed  or the version you have installed was not built with
       the relevant PNM device drivers, ppssttooppnnmm will fail.  You can see if you
       have  the  proper environment by issuing the command gs --help .  If it
       responds and lists under "Available Devices" ppbbmm, ppbbmmrraaww, ppggmm,  ppggmmrraaww,
       ppnnmm, ppnnmmrraaww, ppppmm, or ppppmmrraaww, you're in business.

       It's important to understand that ppssttooppnnmm is a Netpbm image file format
       converter only in the broadest sense of the word, because Postscript is
       far  from an image file format.  What ppssttooppnnmm really is is a Postscript
       renderer - an image generator.  One place you'll notice the  difference
       is  where  you  expect pstopnm | pnmtops to be idempotent (which is not
       the case).  There are details on this kind of conversion below.

       ppssttooppnnmm uses the value of the GGHHOOSSTTSSCCRRIIPPTT environment variable  as  the
       file  name  for  the  Ghostscript  program.  If GGHHOOSSTTSSCCRRIIPPTT is not set,
       ppssttooppnnmm searches your PPAATTHH for a regular file named ggss.  If it  doesn't
       find one, it assumes Ghostscript is in the file //uussrr//bbiinn//ggss.

       ppssttooppnnmm does not use the Netpbm libraries to generate the output files,
       so may not be entirely consistent with most Netpbm programs.

       _p_s_f_i_l_e[..ppss] is the name of the input file.  ppssttooppnnmm will add the ppss  to
       the end of the name you specify if no file exists by the exact name you
       specify, but one with ..ppssadded does.  For Standard Input, use -- or just
       don't give any argument.

       If  you  use  the --ssttddoouutt  option or your input is from Standard Input,
       ppssttooppnnmm outputs images of all the pages as a multi-image file to  Stan‐
       dard  Output.  Otherwise, ppssttooppnnmm creates one file for each page in the
       Postscript input.  The files are named as follows: If the input file is
       named  ppssffiillee..ppss,  the  name  of  the  files will be ppssffiillee000011..ppppmm, ppss‐‐
       ffiillee000022..ppppmm, etc.  The filetype suffix is ..ppppmm, ..ppggmm, or ..ppbbmm,  depend‐
       ing  on  which  kind of output you choose with your invocation options.
       If the input file name does not end in ..ppss, the whole file name is used
       in  the  output file name.  For example, if the input file is named ppss‐‐
       ffiillee..oolldd, the output file name is ppssffiillee..oolldd000011..ppppmm, etc.

       Note that the output file selection is inconsistent  with  most  Netpbm
       programs,  because it does not default to Standard Output.  This is for
       historical reasons, based on the fact that the Netpbm formats  did  not
       always provide for a sequence of images in a single file.

       When  your input is from Standard Input, you may feed multiple Encapsu‐
       lated Postscript documents, one after another, and ppssttooppnnmm converts ev‐
       ery  document  and places it in the Standard Output stream as an image.
       But if your input is from a named file, ppssttooppnnmm expects the file to  be
       an Encapsulated Postscript file, which means it contains only one Enap‐
       sulated Postscript document.  If the file  contains  multiple  concate‐
       nated  documents,  ppssttooppnnmm  ignores every document but the first.  This
       distinction does not exist for non-EPSF Postscript input - ppssttooppnnmm gen‐
       erates an output image for each page of the input regardless of whether
       the input is from Standard Input or from a named file.

       Note that you can generate both kinds of files - concatenated EPSF  and
       multi-page non-EPSF - with ppnnmmttooppss, selecting with the --sseettppaaggee option.

       Each  output  image contains a rectangular area of the page to which it
       pertains.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩    for  details  on
       what  part of the input image goes into the output image and how big it
       is in the output and what borders and margins are in the output image.

       It has been reported that on some Postscript Version  1  input,  Ghost‐
       script, and therefore ppssttooppnnmm, produces no output.  To solve this prob‐
       lem, you can convert the file to Postscript Version 3 with the  program
       ppss22ppss.  It is reported that the program ppssttooppss does not work.


   DDiimmeennssiioonnss
       This  section  describes  what part of the input image gets used in the
       output and the dimensions of the output, including  borders  and  back‐
       ground.

       Note that an output image is associated with a single input page.

       ppssttooppnnmm  starts by taking a rectangular area from the input page.  That
       is called the subject image.

       ppssttooppnnmm may add borders to the subject image to form what is called the
       bordered subject image.

       ppssttooppnnmm  places  the bordered subject image in the center of the output
       image and clips the edges as necessary to fit the computed output image
       size.

       The  location  of the subject image in the Postscript input page is de‐
       fined by four numbers, the lower left corner and the upper right corner
       x  and  y  coordinates.  These coordinates are usually specified by the
       BoundingBox DSC statement (a  Postscript  comment)  in  the  PostScript
       file,  but they can be overridden by the user by specifying one or more
       of the following options: --llllxx, --llllyy, --uurrxx, and --uurryy.

       The presence and thickness of a border to be added to the subject image
       to  form the bordered subject image is controlled by the options --xxbboorr‐‐
       ddeerr and --yybboorrddeerr.  If ppssttooppnnmm does not find a BoundingBox statement  in
       the  input, and you don't specify image area coordinates on the command
       line, ppssttooppnnmm uses default values.  If your input is from Standard  In‐
       put,  ppssttooppnnmm does not use the BoundingBox values (because of the tech‐
       nical difficulty of extracting that information and still  feeding  the
       file  to Ghostscript), so you either have to specify the image area co‐
       ordinates or take the default.

       The output image size is a confusing  thing.   In  a  Postscript  file,
       things  have spatial dimensions.  For example, a particular line may be
       3 centimeters long.  A Postscript printer is supposed to print the line
       3  centimeters  long, using however many pixels that takes, without re‐
       gard to how big the sheet of paper on which it is printing  is.   In  a
       PNM  image,  by contrast, there is no spatial dimension; there are only
       pixels.  You might have a line that is 100 pixels long, but the PNM im‐
       age says nothing about how long that line should be on a printed page.

       ppssttooppnnmm  fills  the  role  of a Postscript printer.  The PNM image is a
       virtual printed page.  ppssttooppnnmm must determine how many pixels  it  will
       use  in  the output image to represent an inch of input image, which is
       the "output device resolution."  Think of it as the number of dots  per
       inch the virtual printer prints on the virtual page.

       The  simplest  thing is for you to tell ppssttooppnnmm exactly what output de‐
       vice resolution to use, using the --ddppii option.  If you say for  example
       --ddppii==330000  and  the  bordered subject image is 2 inches by 3 inches, the
       PNM output will be 600 pixels by 900 pixels.  Using  this  method,  the
       output device resolution has to be the same in both directions.

       Or you can set the output image dimensions with --xxssiizzee and --yyssiizzee.  For
       example, if you say --xxssiizzee==11220000 --yyssiizzee==11880000 and  the  bordered  subject
       image  is  2  inches wide by 3 inches high, the output image is 1200 by
       1800 pixels, with each pixel representing 1/600 inch of input image.

       In the unlikely event that you want different output device resolutions
       in  the two directions, you could use --xxssiizzee and --yyssiizzee to do that.  In
       the above example, if you change --yyssiizzee to 900, a  pixel  still  repre‐
       sents 1/600 inch horizontally, but 1/300 inch vertically.

       If  you specify one of --xxssiizzee and --yyssiizzee and not the other, ppssttooppnnmm de‐
       faults the other such that the output device resolution is the same  in
       both directions.

       The  "x"  and "y" of --xxssiizzee and --yyssiizzee refer to the image being printed
       on the page, not the page.  So if ppssttooppnnmm prints it in landscape orien‐
       tation,  "x"  would pertain to the vertical direction on the page, i.e.
       the vertical direction in the output PNM image.

       If you specify neither the output size nor the  output  device  resolu‐
       tion,  ppssttooppnnmm does some weird computation which exists mainly for his‐
       torical reasons:

       If you specify --nnooccrroopp, ppssttooppnnmm uses the values of --xxmmaaxx and --yymmaaxx  for
       the  output image dimensions.  These default to 612 and 792 pixels, re‐
       spectively.

       The final case, the default, is where you don't  specify  any  size  or
       resolution  options or --nnooccrroopp.  This is the most complicated case.  In
       this case, ppssttooppnnmm first chooses an output device resolution that would
       generate  the  number  of  pixels indicated by --xxmmaaxx and --yymmaaxx from the
       bordered subject image.  Then, based on that resolution, it chooses  an
       output  image size that is just large enough to accommodate the subject
       image (no borders).  Remember (above) that ppssttooppnnmm trims the  edges  of
       the bordered subject image to fit the computed output size.



   GGhhoossttssccrriipptt LLiimmiittaattiioonnss
       Tests  done  in  2013 with Ghostscript 8.71 indicate that Ghostscript's
       ppggmmrraaww output driver has some kind of rounding error  that  causes  the
       pixel  values  to change slightly, and that means ppssttooppnnmm generates in‐
       correct output when you have monochrome  Postscript  input.   But  with
       color Postscript input, ppssttooppnnmm uses Ghostscript's ppppmmrraaww output driver
       and generates correct PPM output.



