OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppssttooppnnmm recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --ffoorrcceeppllaaiinn
               forces the output file to be in plain  (text)  format.   Other‐
              wise,  it is in raw (binary) format.  See ppbbmm(1), etc.  Use this
              instead of the --ppllaaiinn common option if  you  need  plain  format
              output.


       --llllxx==_b_x
              selects  _b_x as the lower left corner x coordinate (in inches) on
              the Postscript  input  page  of  the  subject  image.   See  the
              Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --llllyy==_b_y
              selects  _b_y as the lower left corner y coordinate (in inches) on
              the Postscript  input  page  of  the  subject  image.   See  the
              Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --llaannddssccaappee
              renders the image in landscape orientation.

              If you specify neither --ppoorrttrraaiitt nor --llaannddssccaappee, ppssttooppnnmm chooses
              the orientation that best fits the image on the output page.

              Landscape means printed sideways on the page,  not  printed  the
              long  way.   Those  are different things if the long edge of the
              page is the top one.


       --ppoorrttrraaiitt
              renders the image in portrait orientation.

              See --llaannddssccaappee.


       --nnooccrroopp
              This option causes ppssttooppnnmm to make the output image exactly  the
              dimensions  of  the bordered subject image.  By default, ppssttooppnnmm
              makes the output image the dimensions  specified  by  --xxmmaaxx  and
              --yymmaaxx.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --ppbbmm

       --ppggmm

       --ppppmm   selects  the  format  of the output file.  By default, all files
              are rendered as PPM.


       --ssttddoouutt
              causes output to go to Standard Output  instead  of  to  regular
              files,  one  per  page  (see description of output files above).
              Use ppnnmmsspplliitt to extract individual pages from Standard Output.


       --uurrxx==_t_x
              selects _t_x as the upper right corner x coordinate (in inches) on
              the  Postscript  input  page  of  the  subject  image.   See the
              Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --uurryy==_t_y
              selects _t_y as the upper right corner y coordinate (in inches) on
              the  Postscript  input  page  of  the  subject  image.   See the
              Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .



       --vveerrbboossee
              prints processing information to stdout.


       --xxbboorrddeerr==_f_r_a_c
              specifies that the left and right borders added to  the  subject
              image are to be _f_r_a_c times the subject image width.  The default
              value is 0.1.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .



       --xxmmaaxx==_x_m_a_x
              specifies that the output image is to be _x_m_a_x pixels wide.   The
              default is 612.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .



       --xxssiizzee==_x_s_i_z_e
              specifies that the output image is to be _x_s_i_z_e pixels wide.  See
              the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --yybboorrddeerr==_f_r_a_c
              specifies that the top and bottom borders added to  the  subject
              image  are  to  be _f_r_a_c times the subject image height.  The de‐
              fault value is 0.1.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .



       --yymmaaxx==_y_m_a_x
              specifies that the output image is to be _y_m_a_x pixels high.   The
              default is 792.  See the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --yyssiizzee==_y_s_i_z_e
              specifies  that the output image is to be _y_m_a_x pixels high.  See
              the Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .


       --ddppii==_d_p_i
              specifies the output device resolution, in dots per inch, of the
              Postscript  printer  that ppssttooppnnmm simulates.  This is the number
              of PNM pixels ppssttooppnnmm generates for each inch of image.  See the
              Dimensions section ⟨#dimensions⟩ .

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.21 (March 2004).


       --tteexxttaallpphhaabbiittss=={11,22,44}
              This controls subsample antialiasing of text.  Antialiasing is a
              form of smoothing that eliminates jagged  edges  on  characters.
              Subsample  antialiasing is a kind of antialiasing that uses sub‐
              pixels in a box, and the value of this option  is  the  size  of
              that  box.   4 gives you the best looking output, while 1 causes
              no antialiasing.  Smaller numbers  make  ppnnmmttooppss  use  less  CPU
              time.

              Pstopnm uses Ghostscript's TTeexxttAAllpphhaaBBiittss parameter for this.

              The default is 4.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.53 (December 2010).  Older ver‐
              sions of ppssttooppnnmm do no antialiasing.





